    "created_at": "2016-10-19T19:05:48Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-10-19T19:05:48Z",
    "body": "Re the second case, \"all realms\" means all realms.  Every realm, including unsynchronized realms, has every registered symbol ever created.  But remember that there's no way to enumerate or query registered symbols, so \"having\" a symbol registered is as meaningless as saying all realms in SpiderMonkey \"have\" all 16**(2**28 - 1) possible strings in them.  You do `Symbol.for(\"foo\")`, the registered \"foo\" symbol is returned as if it always existed.  You can't tell that it did or didn't before.  And for unsynced realms, they can never compare two registered symbols _except_ possibly via this channel.  \n\nThe basic idea of cloning a registered symbol would be just a tuple `('RegisteredSymbol, \"description\")` where `\"description\"` is the string originally passed to `Symbol.for`.  There's no deep meaning to it.\n\nSending built-in symbols like `Symbol.iterator` would just be a tuple `(BuiltinSymbol, \"iterator\")` or whatever.\n\nSending any other symbol isn't really practical, because `Symbol(\"foo\") !== Symbol(\"foo\")` means you'd have to have a tuple `(GenericSymbol, \"foo\", <unique identifier>)` to disambiguate same-description symbols, and then you have to start having stable, unique identifiers for such symbols, and it turns into extra memory footprint.\n\nAs to the logic of any of this, I confess I don't much understand the logic (or lack thereof) behind any particular decision as to what structured cloning supports or does not support.  So I wouldn't even know how to respond to a question asking about \"use cases\", any more than I could say what the use case is for why anyone would send a cyclic object graph (or why the inevitable esoteric use cases for the obscure feature are worth accommodating).\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
